# Audit reported map-wide LOD/geometry overlaps before removing any vertices;…

Audit reported map-wide LOD/geometry overlaps before removing any vertices;
permit only deterministic source-backed conversion corrections.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Audit reported map-wide LOD/geometry overlaps before removing any
  vertices; permit only deterministic source-backed conversion corrections.

## Dependencies

No explicit task dependency was recorded in the legacy TODO.

## Evidence

The current world conversion has no map-wide overlap-removal pass. Normal
world meshes use the strict authored-topology loader, and its retained
`discarded_degenerate_triangles` compatibility count is always zero. Layout
validation explicitly permits source overlap and rejects only malformed or
non-finite bounds rather than deleting geometry by distance, extent, or LOD.

The only production duplicate-triangle suppression is scoped to fused interior
ownership. It compares exact source-authored face identity, including mesh and
primitive-group identity, shader, topology, positions, UVs, normals, colors,
and winding. Nearby surfaces, alternate triangulation, distinct material or UV
presentation, reversed winding, uncovered planar spans, and the authored
Halloween overlay remain distinct. All 19 classified interior source packages
retain stable interior identities under the same policy.

Fresh stable-Rust regressions passed all 12 interior ownership tests and both
world-layout tests. The full 1,084-test pipeline suite also passed on the same
committed implementation. This bounds any permissible geometry removal to exact
source-backed duplicate faces; it does not justify deleting reported visual
overlap merely because geometry is nearby or coincident in presentation.

The duplicate-shader publication blocker is now resolved from shipped Pure3D
inventory semantics: same-section shader collisions are first-store-wins, and
the terrain-world ledger proves that authoritative first occurrences precede
their consumers. A fresh 129-package replay then exposed an unrelated
`shrubRound.png` scratch-name collision between independent shader resolutions.
Per-shader scratch isolation preserves both differing payloads and advances the
replay beyond that staging-only blocker.

Across all 129 selected terrain-world packages, 9,765 decoded meshes contain
2,163 triangle-list primitive groups. Twelve groups contain 28 repeated-index
triangle records, including `[786, 782, 782]` and `[794, 794, 785]` in
`common_gens203Shape_054`. The source-preserving analysis path now retains all
such authored records while ordinary FBX mesh loading remains strict.

Shipped Pure3D independently requires that choice for render and intersect data.
`tPrimGroupLoader::LoadStreamed` stores serialized render indices unchanged,
while `IntersectLoader` and `IntersectDSG::mTri` preserve exact intersect
triples.
The replay now advances through both topology boundaries without counting those
records as discarded.

The next fail-closed replay boundary is instead material identity in `l3z3`.
Mesh `z3_gens108Shape_071` alone references `lambert1`, but the package contains
no local shader by that identity. The map-wide audit remains open because the
pipeline deliberately refuses to invent a default material without runtime
inventory evidence proving which external shader is authoritative.
